title: Editing metarecords in the GUI
tags: GUI Metarecord
kind: guide
aliases: bulk-edit m-keys edit-metarecord
summary: Change the fields of one metarecord, of the checked ones or of everything a query matches — by row, from the command input with the m keys, or with a form.

Three ways, for three scopes. All of them write through the daemon, so each change is
in the event log and <<key "log:undo" Ctrl+z>> takes it back ([[Undo and redo]]).

!! One row: the detail panel

In [[metarecord-detail panel]], put the cursor on a row and press
<<key "metarecord:row-edit" Enter>> to change its value,
<<key "metarecord:row-delete" d>> to delete it, <<key "metarecord:open-add-field" a>>
to add one. This is the way to act on ''one particular row'' — one of two equal
values, one `nothing` among several.

!! One metarecord: `metarecord:field`

`metarecord:field <operation> <field> …` acts on the metarecord shown in the detail
panel, which is the selected one. Everything is asked in the command input, with
completion, and what is given on the line is not asked
([[Interactive command arguments]]) — so the same command is a key, a few prompts, or
one line: `metarecord:field set rating 5`. The `m` keys work from the list and the
detail alike; from the list, the detail panel is brought on screen first, so that the
change is seen ([[Commands]]).

|!Key |!Operation |!Does |
|<<key "metarecord:field set" "m s">> |`set` |gives the field this value, in place of every value it had |
|<<key "metarecord:field add" "m a">> |`add` |adds a value to the field; one it already holds changes nothing ([[No duplicate rows]]) |
|<<key "metarecord:field edit" "m v">> |`edit` |changes one value: the field, ''which'' value when it has several, then the new one, starting from the old |
|<<key "metarecord:field remove" "m d">> |`remove` |removes a value — every row equal to it |
|<<key "metarecord:field unset" "m u">> |`unset` |removes the field altogether: it becomes unknown, which is not `nothing` ([[Nothing versus unknown]]) |
|<<key "metarecord:field rename" "m n">> |`rename` |renames the field on this metarecord, all its values with it |
|<<key "metarecord:field retype" "m t">> |`retype` |converts its values to another type, on this metarecord |

Each of them is one revision, however many rows it touches — a `rename` or a `retype`
of a field holding five values is undone in one step ([[Query endpoints]]). `rename`
onto a name the metarecord already has adds the values to it. To retype a field in
the whole repository, use `mf retype` or the [[repos panel]]'s ''Retype…''
([[Changing a field's type]]).

!! Several metarecords: `metarecord:bulk`

`metarecord:bulk <target> <operation> …` does the same over a set, in one revision
([[Editing a set of metarecords]]). The target comes first and is never guessed:

* `selection` — the checked metarecords ([[Cross-panel selection]]);
* `query` — everything the list shows, its finder included; every metarecord of the repository when the list has no query.

<<key "metarecord:bulk" "m b">> asks for it, saying what each would act on
(`selection = 3 checked · query = 1204 matching`) and proposing `selection` when
something is checked. An empty selection is nothing to do; it never falls back on the query.

|!Key |!Operation |
|<<key "metarecord:bulk selection set" "m m s">> |`set` a field on the checked metarecords |
|<<key "metarecord:bulk selection add" "m m a">> |`add` a value |
|<<key "metarecord:bulk selection remove" "m m v">> |`remove` a value |
|<<key "metarecord:bulk selection unset" "m m r">> |`unset` a field |
|<<key "metarecord:bulk selection delete" "m m d">> |`delete` the metarecords themselves; their files stay on disk |

Every operation asks before it writes, giving the number of metarecords it will
change — the checked ones as much as a query's matches.

The same with the mouse: ''Edit / delete…'' in the footer of
[[metarecord-list panel]] opens a form — target, operation, field, type and value
([[Field editor]]). Its target starts on ''Query shown'', and it asks before writing
as well.

!! Typing a value

The value is typed on one line, in the form its type reads ([[Field editor]]). Two
things make that workable for references:

* a `tree_ref` is typed as a path in its tree, completed over the tree's paths: `music/jazz`;
* a `ref` is typed as the name its seed rule gives the target — a tag by its path — and completed over the rule's candidates, <<key "completion:cycle forward" "Ctrl+,">> going through its columns ([[Ref value seeds]], [[Completion views]]). A uuid is always accepted.

What `edit` and `remove` offer are the values the metarecord ''has'', read back the
same way, `∅` standing for an explicit `nothing`. `remove` on the checked or matching
metarecords offers the rule's candidates instead: its value is on no single
metarecord.

!! The type is asked only when nothing gives it

A value is read as the field's type: the one its rows have on this metarecord, else
the one the field has in the repository or in the schema
([[One value type per field name]]). When neither says, the type is asked ''before''
the value — it decides how the value is read — and completed over the types. It is
never assumed to be `string`.

On one line, the type goes between the field and the value, and only where it would
have been asked: `metarecord:field set rating int 5` for a field nobody has yet,
`metarecord:field set tag jazz` for one whose type is known. A line that gives no
type for a field nothing settles is refused.

`retype` always takes a type, since naming one is what it does.

A field whose only value is `nothing` is a field like any other here: its name is
completed, `remove` names its value `∅`, `rename` takes it along. Having no value, it
has no type either: `edit` on it asks for one when the repository knows none, and
`retype` asks for the value that goes with the new type.

!! Reserved fields

Writing an `mfr_*` field needs force ([[Reserved fields]]). The `m` commands and the
forms apply it by themselves when the field named is one — naming it is the intent.
Only the rows of the detail panel are locked, until its ''force'' box is checked.
